  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Increasing knowledge and capacity of LEB farmers to adopt N-GAP</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Lebanon has been struggling with a web of economic and political crises that rapidly worsened since late 2019 and led to raging unemployment and hyperinflation. Lebanon&#x2019;s limited internal production capacity and focus on services sector results in a heavy reliance on imports for products.Agriculture, forestry and fishing make a limited contribution to Lebanon&#x2019;s gross domestig product (GDP) but employ 20&#x2013;25% of the active labour force and contribute around 17% of total exports. During recent decades, the Lebanese government never made agriculture a priority. As a result, the sector received on average less than 1% of overall government spending. Meanwhile Lebanon relies for 80% on food imports. The Netherlands Embassy, amongst others, made substantial investments in the Lebanese agricultural sector during recent years, with programs focusing on various food value chains. A recent agro-food sector analysis highlighted the implementation of National-Good Agricultural Practices (N-GAP) for high potential crops as a key intervention to address issues of low prices for Lebanese agricultural produce in its domestic market. N-GAP would prepare farmers to increase quality and traceability and eventually income by jointly accessing regional markets.  This project serves to train and certify farmers in adopting and applying N-GAP as part of the pilot in Lebanon. This national certification and traceability system will help producers, institutions and main stakeholders improve the efficiency and competitiveness of domestic production in a sustainable way. And lastly it will help consumers access certified, traceable food from Lebanon.</narrative>
  </description>
